        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The <strong>magnitude</strong> of a complex number is its distance from the origin. It&#x27;s denoted as <span class=\"math\">[MATH: |z|.]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "The magnitude of a complex number is its distance from the origin. It's denoted as [MATH: |z|.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, consider the complex number <span class=\"math\">[MATH: z=-4+5\\textrm{i},]</span> shown in the complex plane below.</p>",
            "readable_text": "For example, consider the complex number [MATH: z=-4+5\\textrm{i},] shown in the complex plane below.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "graphic",
            "normalized_html": "<p><img src=\"/graphics/8db46a9aac50b0c9b767f7883c71e174\"></p>",
            "readable_text": "[IMG: /graphics/8db46a9aac50b0c9b767f7883c71e174]",
            "images": [
              {
                "src": "/graphics/8db46a9aac50b0c9b767f7883c71e174",
                "alt": ""
              }
            ],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Using the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: |z||= sqrt((\u2212 4)^{2} + (5)^{2}); = sqrt(16 + 25); = sqrt(41).]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Using the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n[MATH: |z||= sqrt((\u2212 4)^{2} + (5)^{2}); = sqrt(16 + 25); = sqrt(41).]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In general, for a complex number <span class=\"math\">[MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},]</span> the magnitude of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: z]</span> can be found as follows:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "In general, for a complex number [MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},] the magnitude of [MATH: z] can be found as follows:\n[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [
          {
            "src": "/graphics/8db46a9aac50b0c9b767f7883c71e174",
            "alt": ""
          }
        ],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nThe magnitude of a complex number is its distance from the origin. It's denoted as [MATH: |z|.]\n\nContent:\nFor example, consider the complex number [MATH: z=-4+5\\textrm{i},] shown in the complex plane below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/8db46a9aac50b0c9b767f7883c71e174]\n\nContent:\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n[MATH: |z||= sqrt((\u2212 4)^{2} + (5)^{2}); = sqrt(16 + 25); = sqrt(41).]\n\nContent:\nIn general, for a complex number [MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},] the magnitude of [MATH: z] can be found as follows:\n[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]"
      },
