    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe magnitude of a complex number is its distance from the origin. It's denoted as [MATH: |z|.]\n\nContent:\nFor example, consider the complex number [MATH: z=-4+5\\textrm{i},] shown in the complex plane below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/8db46a9aac50b0c9b767f7883c71e174]\n\nContent:\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n[MATH: |z||= sqrt((\u2212 4)^{2} + (5)^{2}); = sqrt(16 + 25); = sqrt(41).]\n\nContent:\nIn general, for a complex number [MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},] the magnitude of [MATH: z] can be found as follows:\n[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Finding the Magnitude of a Complex Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind [MATH: \\left|z\\right|] if [MATH: z=3 - 4\\textrm{i}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFor a complex number [MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},] the magnitude of [MATH: z] is given by\n[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} .]\n\nFor the complex number [MATH: z=3-4\\textrm{i},] we have [MATH: a=3] and [MATH: b=-4.] So, the magnitude is given by\n\n[MATH: |z||= sqrt((3)^{2} + (\u2212 4)^{2}); = sqrt(9 + 16); = sqrt(25); = 5.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: |z|] if [MATH: z = \u2212 10 \u2212 5\\textrm{i}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(120)]\nb. [MATH: 5sqrt(5)]\nc. [MATH: 10sqrt(5)]\nd. [MATH: 125]\ne. [MATH: 15]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: |z|] if [MATH: z = 1 \u2212 sqrt(5) \u2212 3\\textrm{i}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 15 + 2sqrt(5)]\nb. [MATH: 15 + sqrt(10)]\nc. [MATH: 15 \u2212 2sqrt(5)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(15 \u2212 2sqrt(5))]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(15 + 2sqrt(5))]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Finding the Magnitude of a Complex Number With No Real Part\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the magnitude of the number [MATH: \\textrm{i}\\sqrt{2}?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe imaginary number [MATH: \\textrm{i} \\sqrt{2}] is equivalent to the complex number [MATH: z = 0 + \\sqrt{2} \\textrm{i}.]\n\nWe know that for a complex number [MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},] the magnitude of [MATH: z] is given by\n[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} .]\n\nSo, its magnitude of [MATH: z=0 + \\sqrt{2} \\textrm{i}] is given by\n[MATH: |z||= sqrt(a^{2} + b^{2}); = sqrt((0)^{2} + (sqrt(2))^{2}); = sqrt(0 + 2); = sqrt(2).]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the magnitude of the number [MATH: z = 4\\textrm{i}?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 4]\nc. [MATH: 2\\textrm{i}]\nd. [MATH: 2]\ne. [MATH: 4\\textrm{i}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the magnitude of the number [MATH: z = \u2212 sqrt(5)\\textrm{i}?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 5]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 5]\nc. [MATH: sqrt(5)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 sqrt(5)]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(\u2212 5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Finding the Magnitude of a Complex Number With Variable Coefficients\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind [MATH: \\left|z\\right|] if [MATH: z=5c - 9d\\textrm{i}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFor a complex number [MATH: z=a+b\\textrm{i},] the magnitude of [MATH: z] is given by\n[MATH: |z| = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} .]\n\nFor the complex number [MATH: z=5c - 9d\\textrm{i},] we have [MATH: a=5c] and [MATH: b=-9d.] So, the magnitude is given by\n\n[MATH: |z||= sqrt((5c)^{2} + (\u2212 9d)^{2}); = sqrt(25c^{2} + 81d^{2}).]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: |z|] if [MATH: z = x + y\\textrm{i}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(x^{2} + y^{2})]\nb. [MATH: sqrt(x + y)]\nc. [MATH: sqrt(x^{2}y^{2})]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(x^{2}) + sqrt(y^{2})]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(x \u2212 y)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: |z|] if [MATH: z = \u2212 c \u2212 d\\textrm{i}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: sqrt(c^{2} + d^{2})]\nb. [MATH: sqrt(c \u2212 d)]\nc. [MATH: sqrt(c + d)]\nd. [MATH: sqrt(c^{2} \u2212 d^{2})]\ne. [MATH: sqrt(c^{2})sqrt(d^{2})]"
  }
